# Manual setup steps: things no script can do (privacy permissions, sign-ins, MDM
# apps, settings that aren't exposed via `defaults`). This file is the single source
# of truth for them. It is read by:
#   scripts/manual-steps.sh list | open | check   (numbered checklist, walk-through, checks)
#   install.sh / bootstrap.sh                      (print the checklist at the end)
#   macconfig-check.sh                                       ("Manual steps" section)
# docs/manual-steps.md is generated by scripts/render-manual-steps-doc.sh
# (the table is `scripts/manual-steps.sh list --markdown`).
#
# Format (pipe-delimited, one step per line):
#   id | group | title | where | open | check
#   id:    unique kebab-case id
#   group: heading the step is listed under (keep a group's steps together)
#   title: what to do, in plain English
#   where: where to do it (System Settings paths use macOS 27 names)
#   open:  what `manual-steps.sh open` opens, or empty:
#            x-apple.systempreferences:<pane>  a System Settings pane
#            https://… | macappstore://…   a web page / the App Store
#            app:<App Name>                 open -a "<App Name>"
#            safari:https://…               open -a Safari <url>, not the default browser
#   check: how `manual-steps.sh check` verifies it (always read-only), or empty = check by hand:
#            tcc:<Service>:<bundle-id>      allowed in the system TCC database (kTCCService<Service>);
#                                           reading it needs Full Disk Access, else check by hand
#            defaults:<domain>:<k=v>[,k=v]  every key reads back as that value
#            app:<path>                     path exists (@HOME@ -> $HOME)
#            logi:<id>[,<id>]               Logi Options+ values match lib/logi-expected.list
#                                           (read from a temp copy of settings.db by
#                                           lib/logi-settings.py; never written)
#            @macconfig:<area>            same read-only check as that macconfig-check.sh
#                                           section (mdm, karabiner, display, power,
#                                           notifications, login-items, finder, modes).
#                                           manualsteps open/check run that step's
#                                           function. macconfig's Manual steps section
#                                           does not count it again.
#            @macconfig-check.sh            legacy: report covered, do not run a section
#            hammerspoon-ax | karabiner-driver | pointer-size | input-british | op-cli | gh-auth
#            | spotlight-results | remove-widgets | home-printer | tailscale-signin
#            | edge-light | whatsapp-web
#                                           named checks. pointer-size, input-british, op-cli,
#                                           gh-auth, and spotlight-results live in
#                                           lib/session-checks.sh, sourced here. macconfig-check.sh
#                                           prints that result in Manual steps and does not run the
#                                           checks again. A warning keeps the step id (Needs Paul).
#                                           --fix never changes them. spotlight-results cannot read
#                                           the toggles, so the result is check by hand, reported
#                                           with the other manual steps.
#                                           remove-widgets reads widgets.instances in the
#                                           notificationcenterui plist (lib/desktop-widgets.py;
#                                           never written, and NotificationCenter / chronod are
#                                           not killed). The Desktop widgets section reports
#                                           it. Manual steps skips that duplicate when the section
#                                           ran. open still uses the check, so the step is shown
#                                           only when a widget is placed. home-printer only reads
#                                           lpstat. It never adds a queue. The HP ENVY 6000 series
#                                           is visible on Bonjour only on the home network.
#                                           tailscale-signin reads `status --json`
#                                           and prints only the state and the
#                                           login name. It never prints keys.
#                                           edge-light only tries to read the
#                                           protected Control Centre video-effects
#                                           file and RingLightHelper's group.
#                                           The file is permission denied and the
#                                           group holds no preferences, so the
#                                           result is check by hand. open always
#                                           shows the step. Nothing is written.
#                                           whatsapp-web: ~/Applications/WhatsApp Web.app
#                                           exists and its CFBundleIdentifier
#                                           starts with com.apple.Safari.WebApp.
#                                           The UUID in that id is not stored.
#                                           Linking the phone cannot be read.
# Blank lines and lines beginning with # are ignored. Fields must not contain '|'.

# ---- Company apps (MDM) ----
mdm-apps|Company apps (MDM)|Install the company apps from Fleet Self service (Fleet Desktop menu bar → My device → Self service), not Homebrew. The names are in lib/mdm-apps.list|Fleet Desktop menu bar → My device → Self service|app:Fleet Desktop|@macconfig:mdm
1password-signin|Company apps (MDM)|Sign in to 1Password and unlock it|1Password app|app:1Password|
1password-cli|Company apps (MDM)|Turn on Integrate with 1Password CLI, then run op signin once in a terminal|1Password → Settings → Developer|app:1Password|op-cli

# ---- Sign-ins ----
gh-auth|Sign-ins|Sign in to the GitHub CLI (needed to clone this private repo)|Terminal: gh auth login||gh-auth
tailscale-signin|Sign-ins|Open Tailscale, turn it on, and sign in as paul.done@anysphere.co|Open Tailscale from Applications (it comes from Fleet Self service) → click the Tailscale icon in the menu bar → click Log in (or turn the Connected switch on) → choose sign in with your work account and use paul.done@anysphere.co → finish sign-in in the browser|app:Tailscale|tailscale-signin
# Tailscale has no launch-at-login option of its own. Open at Login is read
# (System Events login-item names, and the BTM app record). It is not added:
# the other apps register themselves, and osascript "make login item" needs
# an Automation prompt. When the read says it is missing, the reason line is
# NOTSET: and the check's click path. When Tailscale.app is not installed yet,
# the step is left alone.
tailscale-login|Sign-ins|Add Tailscale to Open at Login. It has no launch-at-login option of its own|System Settings → General → Login Items & Extensions → under Open at Login click + → Applications → Tailscale → Open|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.LoginItems-Settings.extension|@macconfig:login-items

# ---- Apps not in the Brewfile ----
cursor-nightly|Apps not in the Brewfile|Install Cursor Nightly (separate app, next to the stable Cursor)|cursor.com/nightlydownload|https://cursor.com/nightlydownload|app:/Applications/Cursor Nightly.app
youtube-music|Apps not in the Brewfile|Install YouTube Music as a Chrome app|Chrome → music.youtube.com → ⋮ → Cast, save, and share → Install page as app|https://music.youtube.com|app:@HOME@/Applications/Chrome Apps.localized/YouTube Music.app
whatsapp-web|Apps not in the Brewfile|Add WhatsApp Web as a Safari web app and link your Android phone|Open Safari → go to https://web.whatsapp.com → File → Add to Dock… (only on a normal web page, not a blank tab or a Private window) → keep the name "WhatsApp Web" and the WhatsApp icon → Add. On the Android phone: WhatsApp → ⋮ → Linked devices → Link a device → scan the QR code. Linking the phone can't be checked. Then run dock.sh, then macconfig --fix, so WhatsApp Web sits between Slack and Granola on Desktop 1.|safari:https://web.whatsapp.com|whatsapp-web

# ---- Privacy & Security permissions ----
hammerspoon-ax|Privacy & Security permissions|Allow Hammerspoon, then restart it and choose Reload Config|Open Hammerspoon first. System Settings → Privacy & Security → Accessibility. If it is not listed, click + and add it from Applications|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.preference.security?Privacy_Accessibility|hammerspoon-ax
karabiner-driver|Privacy & Security permissions|Turn on the Karabiner driver|Open Karabiner-Elements first so it asks for its driver. Then System Settings → General → Login Items & Extensions: scroll to Extensions, choose By Category, click ⓘ next to Driver Extensions, and turn on .Karabiner-VirtualHIDDevice-Manager (org.pqrs.Karabiner-DriverKit-VirtualHIDDevice). Enter your password if asked, then click Done|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.LoginItems-Settings.extension|karabiner-driver
karabiner-ax|Privacy & Security permissions|Allow Karabiner-Elements and Karabiner-Core-Service|Open Karabiner-Elements first. System Settings → Privacy & Security → Accessibility. If it is not listed, click + and add it from Applications|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.preference.security?Privacy_Accessibility|tcc:Accessibility:org.pqrs.Karabiner-Core-Service
karabiner-login-items|Privacy & Security permissions|Allow Karabiner-Elements to run in the background. It is not changed automatically.|Open Karabiner-Elements first. System Settings → General → Login Items & Extensions → Allow in the Background. Karabiner-Core-Service and Karabiner-VirtualHIDDevice-Daemon should be enabled and allowed. Karabiner-Elements Privileged Daemons v2 may stay disabled|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.LoginItems-Settings.extension|@macconfig:karabiner
raycast-ax|Privacy & Security permissions|Allow Raycast for Clipboard History|Open Raycast first. System Settings → Privacy & Security → Accessibility. If it is not listed, click + and add it from Applications|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.preference.security?Privacy_Accessibility|tcc:Accessibility:com.raycast.macos
ghostty-fda|Privacy & Security permissions|Give Ghostty Full Disk Access|Open Ghostty first. System Settings → Privacy & Security → Full Disk Access → Ghostty. This lets macconfig read Login Items and Finder Recents|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.preference.security?Privacy_AllFiles|tcc:SystemPolicyAllFiles:com.mitchellh.ghostty
grokbot-fda|Privacy & Security permissions|Give Grok Bot Full Disk Access|Open Grok Bot first. System Settings → Privacy & Security → Full Disk Access → Grok Bot. Needed for its unattended macconfig runs|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.preference.security?Privacy_AllFiles|tcc:SystemPolicyAllFiles:com.anysphere.sand

# ---- Trackpad, mouse & pointer ----
# Tap to click, click pressure, secondary click, three-finger drag, tracking speed,
# and Force Click (On) are lib/macos-defaults.list. A change may need a logout/login.
# Look up & data detectors is NSGlobalDomain com.apple.trackpad.forceClick = 0.
# That is a check only. ActuateDetents and ForceSuppressed stay in the defaults
# list (Force Click and haptic feedback On) and are a different setting.
trackpad-lookup|Trackpad, mouse & pointer|Trackpad: Look up & data detectors Off|System Settings → Trackpad → Point & Click|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Trackpad-Settings.extension|defaults:NSGlobalDomain:com.apple.trackpad.forceClick=0
# Dragging lives in Accessibility → Pointer Control → Trackpad Options…, a sheet,
# not a page of its own. defaults write of Dragging/DragLock does not reliably
# change that sheet, so this stays a check only. Paul wants dragging On, without drag lock.
trackpad-dragging|Trackpad, mouse & pointer|Use trackpad for dragging On (Without Drag Lock)|System Settings → Accessibility → Pointer Control → click Trackpad Options… (opens a sheet) → Use trackpad for dragging On (Without Drag Lock) → Done|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Accessibility-Settings.extension|defaults:com.apple.AppleMultitouchTrackpad:Dragging=1,DragLock=0
pointer-size|Trackpad, mouse & pointer|Pointer size one notch above Normal|System Settings → Accessibility → Display → Pointer → Pointer size|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Accessibility-Settings.extension|pointer-size
# Tracking speed and scrolling speed are readable, but defaults write does not
# reliably drive System Settings → Mouse on recent macOS, so they stay a check
# only (warn on drift; macconfig-check.sh --fix does not write them).
# Verified on pdone-mac2: com.apple.mouse.scaling 1.5 is the 7th notch from Slow
# (1.0 was the 5th); com.apple.scrollwheel.scaling 0.5 is the 5th notch from Slow
# (0.3125 was the 4th). The double-click threshold key is unset on this Mac, and
# secondary click has no separate reliable key, so the step says to leave those,
# and Natural scrolling, as they are (Logi Options+ sets the wheel direction).
mouse-settings|Trackpad, mouse & pointer|Set Tracking speed to the 7th notch from Slow and Scrolling Speed to the 5th notch from Slow. Leave Double-Click Speed, Click Right Side and Natural scrolling as they are (Logi Options+ sets the wheel direction).|System Settings → Mouse|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Mouse-Settings.extension|defaults:NSGlobalDomain:com.apple.mouse.scaling=1.5,com.apple.scrollwheel.scaling=0.5
logi-smooth-scrolling|Trackpad, mouse & pointer|Logi Options+: Smooth scrolling On (main wheel and thumb wheel)|Logi Options+ → MX Master 3S → Point & Scroll|app:logioptionsplus|logi:wheel-smooth,thumb-smooth
logi-cloud-backup|Trackpad, mouse & pointer|Logi Options+: sign in to your Logi account first and turn on cloud backup (Automatically backup all devices); confirm it shows a recent Last backup. Use Restore settings from backup (same page) on a new Mac if needed|Logi Options+ → MX Master 3S → Settings → Other → Automatically backup all devices|app:logioptionsplus|

# ---- Display, keyboard & desktop ----
# Automatically adjust brightness is not scripted. Turning it off needs an
# admin password, and the saved value is the root-owned CoreBrightness plist.
# bootstrap.sh calls this step out when it prints the manual steps. open jumps
# to System Settings → Displays. True Tone and battery dim stay as they are.
auto-brightness|Display, keyboard & desktop|Automatically adjust brightness Off. This stays by hand: it needs an admin password and the saved setting is root-owned. Leave True Tone and Slightly dim the display on battery as they are|System Settings → Displays|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Displays-Settings.extension|@macconfig:display
# More Space is _spdisplays_resolution starting with the value for this panel
# size in lib/display-resolution.list (14" 1800 x 1169, 16" 2056 x 1329).
# macos.sh sets it with displayplacer on the built-in panel. An unlisted
# panel size or a clamshell Mac (no built-in panel) is an info skip. If
# displayplacer is missing or the mode is not listed, set it by hand here.
display-more-space|Display, keyboard & desktop|Built-in display: More Space. macos.sh sets this with displayplacer when that mode is listed for the built-in panel (14 inch 1800x1169, 16 inch 2056x1329). Other displays are left as they are. If displayplacer is missing or the mode is not listed, set it by hand|System Settings → Displays|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Displays-Settings.extension|@macconfig:display
# The ARZOPA 16.1" portable monitor. Not connected is an info skip, not drift.
# macos.sh pins 1920x1080, scaling off, origin (-1920,0), at 180Hz, or at the
# highest rate offered at 1920x1080 when 180Hz is not (through a dock), and keeps the
# laptop at (0,0) so the Dock and menu bar stay on it. Matched by name or
# serial, not by the persistent screen id. A missing mode stays by hand.
arzopa-display|Display, keyboard & desktop|ARZOPA portable monitor, when it is plugged in: 1920x1080, scaling off, to the left of the laptop with the tops aligned. The refresh rate is 180Hz when the monitor is plugged straight into the Mac. Through a dock that does not offer 180Hz, it is the highest rate the dock offers (144Hz on the StarTech Thunderbolt 5 dock). The laptop stays the main display so the Dock and menu bar stay on it. macos.sh sets this with displayplacer when that mode is listed. When the monitor is not connected, nothing is wrong. If displayplacer is missing or the mode is not listed, set it by hand|System Settings → Displays|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Displays-Settings.extension|@macconfig:display
# Tahoe Night (Landscape) is a wallpaper-catalog asset, not a file under
# /System/Library/Desktop Pictures. System Events "set picture" cannot select
# that asset, and the wallpaper store is not written.
wallpaper|Display, keyboard & desktop|Wallpaper: Tahoe Night (Landscape). Set it by hand. There is no reliable command for this system picture (it is a wallpaper-catalog asset, not a file), and the wallpaper store is not written. macconfig warns if it is a different picture|System Settings → Wallpaper|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Wallpaper-Settings.extension|@macconfig:display
# Mains power: sleep 0, display sleep 10 minutes, wake for network access on.
# Battery: display sleep 2 minutes. Battery idle sleep is not changed.
# Closing the lid still sleeps the Mac. The screen still locks.
# macconfig-check.sh reads `pmset -g custom` and warns. --fix does not run sudo.
# macos.sh may run the sudo pmset commands and skips them when sudo is not available.
ac-sleep|Display, keyboard & desktop|On mains power the Mac does not sleep on its own, the display sleeps after 10 minutes, and wake for network access is on. On battery the display sleeps after 2 minutes. Closing the lid still sleeps the Mac. Battery idle sleep is not changed|Terminal: sudo pmset -c sleep 0; sudo pmset -c displaysleep 10; sudo pmset -b displaysleep 2; sudo pmset -c womp 1||@macconfig:power
# Charge Limit 80% (macOS Tahoe 26.4 or later, Apple silicon laptops).
# Apple documents this only in System Settings. There is no pmset or defaults
# write. The Shortcuts action Set Battery Charge Limit is not a shell command.
# SMC writers are a different mechanism and are not this setting.
# The chosen percentage cannot be read without the private entitlement
# com.apple.private.iokit.soc-limit. pmset -g, pmset -g battlimit,
# ioreg -rn AppleSmartBattery, system_profiler SPPowerDataType, and the
# power preferences under /Library/Preferences and ~/Library do not show it.
# Charge Limit is not a row on the Battery page. It is on the sheet opened by
# ⓘ next to Charging, with Optimised Battery Charging.
# A desktop has no battery. Leave the check empty. Do not add a check that
# only warns when a key is missing.
charge-limit|Display, keyboard & desktop|Charge Limit 80% (Apple silicon laptops).|System Settings → Battery → click ⓘ next to Charging → drag Charge Limit to 80% (leave Optimised Battery Charging on) → Done|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Battery-Settings.extension|
input-british|Display, keyboard & desktop|Keyboard input source: British|System Settings → Keyboard → Text Input → click Edit… next to Input Sources (opens a sheet) → British → Done|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Keyboard-Settings.extension|input-british
# Hiding widgets is lib/macos-defaults.list (StandardHideWidgets,
# StageManagerHideWidgets). That does not delete placed widgets. They live in
# the notificationcenterui plist (widgets → instances) and still appear when
# the desktop is revealed. The check only reads that plist. macconfig --fix
# does not write it and does not kill NotificationCenter or chronod.
# Click wallpaper to reveal desktop stays out.
remove-widgets|Display, keyboard & desktop|Remove every widget from the desktop: Control-click (or right-click) each widget → Remove Widget.|Desktop (reveal the desktop if the widgets are hidden)||remove-widgets
user-picture|Display, keyboard & desktop|Set your user picture to the Dog. A different picture is a warning. macconfig does not change it|System Settings → Users & Groups → your account picture|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Users-Groups-Settings.extension|@macconfig:display

# ---- Video calls ----
# Edge Light lights the edges of the screen like a ring light while the camera
# is on. Paul wants it Off. On pdone-mac2 the on/off state is in
# ~/Library/Group Containers/group.com.apple.secure-control-center-preferences/Library/Preferences/group.com.apple.secure-control-center-preferences.av.plist
# and reading that file is permission denied. RingLightHelper's group
# (group.com.apple.ringlight) holds no preferences. The check only tries to
# read. It never writes. Because the state cannot be read, the step is always
# shown. The why-line is MANUAL: Can't be checked, so always shown. macconfig
# uses that same line. open launches Photo Booth so the camera is on
# and Video Effects appears in Control Centre.
edge-light|Video calls|Edge Light Off|Start a video call (or open Photo Booth / FaceTime) so the camera is on → click Control Centre in the menu bar → Video Effects → click Edge Light so it is Off|app:Photo Booth|edge-light

# ---- Printer ----
# Read-only: lpstat -p, or lpstat -v if -p does not name the queue.
# Nothing here adds a printer. The Bonjour name only appears on the home network.
home-printer|Printer|Add the home printer HP ENVY 6000 series|System Settings → Printers & Scanners → click Add Printer, Scanner or Fax… → Default tab → choose HP ENVY 6000 series (Bonjour Multifunction, only shown when on the home network) → leave Use: HP ENVY 6000 series → Add|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Print-Scan-Settings.extension|home-printer

# ---- Notifications & Spotlight ----
# Notification Centre is the block at the top of System Settings → Notifications.
# Show previews, when display is sleeping, when screen is locked, and when
# mirroring or sharing the display are not per-app flags. Per-app
# content_visibility is that app's own preview (Default, Never, When Unlocked,
# Always), not the Show previews menu. ~/Library/Preferences/com.apple.ncprefs.plist
# is not the macOS 27 store. No key here has been shown to match these four
# controls. Leave the checks empty. The why-line is
# MANUAL: Can't check this automatically. If it's already set, press s.
# macconfig uses that same line. Do not warn because a guessed key is missing,
# and do not write a plist.
notifications-previews|Notifications & Spotlight|Show previews: When Unlocked|System Settings → Notifications → Notification Center → click the Show previews menu → When Unlocked|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Notifications-Settings.extension|
notifications-display-sleep|Notifications & Spotlight|When display is sleeping off|System Settings → Notifications → Notification Center → turn off when display is sleeping|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Notifications-Settings.extension|
notifications-lock-screen|Notifications & Spotlight|When screen is locked on|System Settings → Notifications → Notification Center → turn on when screen is locked|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Notifications-Settings.extension|
notifications-mirroring|Notifications & Spotlight|Notifications Off when mirroring or sharing the display|System Settings → Notifications → Notification Center → click the menu for when mirroring or sharing the display → Notifications Off|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Notifications-Settings.extension|
# Bundle ids live in lib/notifications-off.list (Calendar, FaceTime, Game Center,
# Home, Tips, Wallet, Messages). macconfig-check.sh reads the usernoted plist
# and never writes it. Messages may say Critical in the list: Allow notifications
# is off and Critical Alerts stay allowed, which counts as off. The four
# Notification Centre rows above stay by hand.
notifications-apps|Notifications & Spotlight|Turn notifications Off for Calendar, FaceTime, Game Center, Home, Tips, Wallet, Messages|System Settings → Notifications → Calendar → turn off Allow notifications. System Settings → Notifications → FaceTime → turn off Allow notifications. System Settings → Notifications → Game Center → turn off Allow notifications. System Settings → Notifications → Home → turn off Allow notifications. System Settings → Notifications → Tips → turn off Allow notifications. System Settings → Notifications → Wallet → turn off Allow notifications. System Settings → Notifications → Messages → turn off Allow notifications. Messages may say Critical in the list: Allow notifications is off and Critical Alerts stay allowed, so leave that as it is|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Notifications-Settings.extension|@macconfig:notifications
spotlight-results|Notifications & Spotlight|Spotlight: turn off results from Books, Calendar, Contacts, Mail, Messages, Music, Notes, Photos, Podcasts, Reminders, Safari, Shortcuts, Stocks, Tips, and Voice Memos. Leave App Store, Calculator, Dictionary, and System Settings on.|System Settings → Spotlight → Results from Apps|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Spotlight-Settings.extension|spotlight-results

# ---- Login Items & Finder ----
login-items-off|Login Items & Finder|Keep ChatGPT Off at login|System Settings → General → Login Items & Extensions|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.LoginItems-Settings.extension|@macconfig:login-items
finder-sidebar|Login Items & Finder|Finder sidebar: the Tags group stays collapsed, Recents and Shared stay hidden, and, when the sidebar files name the row, AirDrop and iCloud Drive are hidden, and your home folder, Macintosh HD, Network, and Trash are visible. A row the files do not name stays by hand. macconfig does not change the sidebar|Finder → Settings → Sidebar|app:Finder|@macconfig:finder
# FavoriteTagNames is not what the sidebar shows. On pdone-mac2 every box
# under Show these tags in the sidebar is unticked and the sidebar has no
# Tags section, but `defaults read com.apple.finder FavoriteTagNames` still
# returns ("", Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Blue, Purple, Grey). Tags sync
# through iCloud (TagsCloudSerialNumber). Do not read that key and do not
# warn. Leave the check empty. The why-line is
# MANUAL: Can't check this automatically. If it's already set, press s.
finder-sidebar-tags|Login Items & Finder|No tags shown in the sidebar|Finder → Settings → Tags → untick every box under Show these tags in the sidebar||

# ---- App settings ----
# Raycast is set by hand: the launcher hotkey, then Clipboard History.
# Do not click Replace Spotlight. That turns off Command+Space (symbolic hotkey 64).
raycast-hotkey|App settings|Raycast hotkey: Shift+Control+Command+R (off Option+Space, which clashes with ChatGPT)|Open Raycast → Settings (Command+,) → General → Raycast Hotkey → click the hotkey recorder and press Shift+Control+Command+R. Don't click the Replace Spotlight button next to it (it turns off Spotlight's Command+Space)|app:Raycast|defaults:com.raycast.macos:raycastGlobalHotkey=Shift-Control-Command-15
raycast-clipboard|App settings|Raycast Clipboard History: hotkey Control+Command+V, keep history 1 day, disable 1Password and 1Password for Safari|Open Raycast, run Clipboard History once and allow Accessibility if asked. Then Raycast Settings → Clipboard History (in the left sidebar; there is no Extensions tab) → Keep history for = 1 Day → under Disabled Applications click + and add 1Password and 1Password for Safari (Keychain Access and Passwords are already listed) → in Commands, on the Clipboard History row click Add Hotkey and press Control+Command+V|app:Raycast|

# ---- Modes (Hammerspoon menu-bar switcher; see docs/modes.md) ----
mode-focus-modes|Modes (menu-bar switcher)|Create two Focus modes, WebConf and DeepWork. WebConf allows Granola. DeepWork allows Hammerspoon, so the end-of-session notification gets through. Allowed People can stay None. An app only appears in that Add list after it has asked macOS for notification permission at least once (open the app and allow notifications first).|System Settings → Focus → click Add Focus… (opens a sheet) → Custom → name WebConf (or DeepWork) → pick any icon → Done → Allowed Apps → Add|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Focus-Settings.extension|@macconfig:modes
mode-focus-shortcuts|Modes (menu-bar switcher)|Create four Shortcuts, each with one Set Focus action. In Shortcuts, click + in the toolbar, click the title at the top and rename it exactly, then in Search Actions on the right type Set Focus and double-click it. Click the blue placeholder words to set them, then close the window to save. The action starts as Turn Do Not Disturb On, so click Do Not Disturb and pick WebConf or DeepWork. Names must match exactly: "Mode WebConf On" is Turn WebConf On until Turned Off; "Mode WebConf Off" is Turn WebConf Off; "Mode DeepWork On" is Turn DeepWork On until Turned Off; "Mode DeepWork Off" is Turn DeepWork Off.|Shortcuts → + → Search Actions "Set Focus"|app:Shortcuts|@macconfig:modes
mode-hammerspoon-alerts|Modes (menu-bar switcher)|Hammerspoon notifications: style Alerts, so the DeepWork end-of-session buttons (Take a break / Back to Normal / Another session) show|System Settings → Notifications → Hammerspoon → Alerts|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Notifications-Settings.extension|@macconfig:notifications
mode-menubar-pin|Modes (menu-bar switcher)|Pin the mode-switcher menu-bar icon so it stays visible: hold Command (⌘) and drag it toward Control Centre / the clock, because icons further from them get auto-hidden behind <<|Menu bar → ⌘-drag the mode icon (desktop computer / red ⏺ / brain)||

# ---- Menu bar ----
# Raycast, Logi Options+ and Docker have no reliable defaults key for this toggle.
# Raycast's own menu-bar switch is not scriptable. Leave the other icons alone.
menubar-third-party-hide|Menu bar|Allow in the Menu Bar: turn Off Raycast, Logi Options+ and Docker. Leave Tailscale, 1Password, Granola, Hammerspoon and Fleet Desktop as they are. Raycast's own menu-bar toggle isn't scriptable|System Settings → Menu Bar → Allow in the Menu Bar|x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.ControlCenter-Settings.extension|
# Control Centre tiles are Edit Controls in the menu-bar Control Centre, not
# the allow-list above and not the @host/com.apple.controlcenter menu-bar
# icon rows. The only readable layout file,
# ~/Library/Preferences/ByHost/com.apple.controlcenter.bentoboxes.<host UUID>.plist,
# keeps a history of module instances in an undocumented nested binary format
# and was not updated when the tiles were removed (on 25 Oct 2026 it still
# listed AirDrop and FocusModes hours after they were gone from Control
# Centre), so it can't tell whether a tile is showing. Leave the check empty.
# Do not add a macconfig check and do not write that plist.
control-centre-controls|Menu bar|Control Centre: remove AirDrop, Stage Manager, Dark Mode, Screenshot and Focus. Keep Screen Mirroring, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, Now Playing, Display and Sound.|Click Control Centre in the menu bar → Edit Controls → remove AirDrop, Stage Manager, Dark Mode, Screenshot and Focus (click the minus on each, or drag it out) → Done. Clicking Done is what saves it.||
